Loan Repayment Prediction: Machine Learning for Alternative Credit Assessment
________________________________________
 Problem Statement
•	Traditional lenders depend heavily on credit scores to assess repayment ability.
•	This excludes underbanked groups (students, unemployed, first-time borrowers) lacking credit history.
•	These groups often resort to high-interest, exploitative lenders.
•	The project aims to predict loan repayment ability using alternative features like employment, income, and payment history — not just credit scores.
•	Goal: Promote financial inclusion through data-driven lending.
________________________________________
Approach
•	Dataset Used: Home Credit Default Risk (307,511 applications, 122 raw features).
•	Steps:
1.	Data Preprocessing:
	Merged 7 data sources using client IDs.
	Dropped columns with >30% missing values.
	Label-encoded categorical variables.
	Normalized engineered features (total 651).
2.	Class Imbalance Mitigation:
	Target class imbalance: 91.9% negative → 8.1% positive.
	Used down-sampling to achieve 1:1 ratio for balanced training.
3.	Multi-Model Evaluation:
	Trained 6 algorithms:
	Logistic Regression
	Decision Tree
	K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)
	Random Forest
	Support Vector Machine (SVM)
	Artificial Neural Network (ANN)
	Dataset split: 39,720 train / 9,930 test samples.
4.	Clustering Analysis:
	Applied K-Means (k=4) to segment borrowers.
	Trained cluster-specific models for tailored predictions.
5.	Feature Importance:
	Extracted top predictors using Random Forest feature importance.
________________________________________
 Implementation Highlights
•	Model Results:
o	Logistic Regression: 69.97% accuracy, 0.75 ROC-AUC (best baseline).
o	Artificial Neural Network (ANN): 69.15% accuracy, 0.76 ROC-AUC.
o	Random Forest: 68.23% accuracy.
•	K-Means Clustering Impact:
o	Overall accuracy improved to 71.57%.
o	Cluster 3 achieved 82.34% accuracy, showing strong group-level differences.
•	Key Predictors:
o	Employment days (NUM_DAYS_EMPLOYED) – strongest predictor.
o	Age (DAYS_BIRTH) – second most important.
o	Income metrics and external source scores also significant.
•	Optimization:
o	SVM trained on 5,000-sample subset for faster computation (15+ min → 2 min).
o	ANN training accelerated using GPU + TensorFlow.
________________________________________
 Technical Implementation
•	Language: Python 3.10
•	Libraries: pandas, scikit-learn, TensorFlow
•	Pipeline:
o	Feature concatenation (7 files)
o	Missing value handling
o	Label encoding
o	Normalization
•	Evaluation Metrics:
o	Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-Score, ROC-AUC
o	Cluster-level metrics for detailed analysis
•	Outputs Generated:
o	Model notebooks
o	ROC curves
o	Feature importance visualizations
o	Technical documentation
________________________________________
 Key Conclusions
1.	Logistic Regression provided the best tradeoff between accuracy, interpretability, and efficiency (69.97%, ROC-AUC 0.75).
2.	Cluster-based models improved accuracy by 1.6%, highlighting borrower diversity.
3.	Employment stability and age are critical predictors for repayment ability.
4.	Machine learning enables inclusive credit decisions beyond traditional credit scores.
________________________________________
Challenges & Limitations
•	Severe class imbalance: 8.1% positive → required down-sampling (data loss risk).
•	Unexplained features (EXT_SOURCE_1/2/3): Highly predictive but poorly documented.
•	Temporal inconsistencies: Aggregated data across inconsistent periods.
•	High dimensionality (651 features): Risk of multicollinearity and high computation time.
•	Limited explainability: ANN models lack interpretability for regulated finance contexts.
________________________________________
Project Summary
•	Dataset: Home Credit Default Risk (Kaggle)
•	Algorithms: 6 models (LogReg, Tree, KNN, RF, SVM, ANN)
•	Best Accuracy: 69.97% (Logistic Regression)
•	Clustered Model Accuracy: 71.57%
•	Top Feature: Days Employed (NUM_DAYS_EMPLOYED)
